      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this OIML certificate for a water meter (WATERFLUX 3070).\n\nDocument-level:\n- Certificate N° R49-1/2006-NL1-12.03\n- Project number 12200373\n- Page total 8\n- Member state: The Netherlands\n- Date issued: 29 November 2012\n- OIML issuer id: NL1 (Issuing Authority NL1)\n\nIssuing authority:\n- Name: NMi Certin B.V.\n- Address: Hugo de Grootplein 1, 3314 EG Dordrecht, The Netherlands\n- Person responsible: C. Oosterman\n- Person title: Head Certification Board\n- Phone: +31 78 6332332\n- Email: certin@nmi.nl\n- Website: www.nmi.nl\n- oiml_issuer_id: NL1\n\nApplicants:\n- KROHNE Altometer, Kerkeplaat 12, 3313 LC Dordrecht, The Netherlands\n\nManufacturers:\n- KROHNE Measurement Technology (Shanghai) Co., Ltd., No. 555 Minshen Road, Songjiang Industrial Zone, Shanghai 201612, China\n\nCertified type:\n- Category: \"Water meter\"\n- Type designations: [\"WATERFLUX 3070\"]\n- Module designation: null\n- Description: Water meter intended for the metering of cold potable water and hot water, model \"WATERFLUX 3070\", class 1 and 2. Based on an electromagnetic principle, designed to measure forward and reverse flow, with (minimum) 0 D straight inlet and outlet, with no flow conditioner and equipped with an electronic calculating/indicating device.\n\nRecommendation: R49, edition 2006, scheme B (OIML-CS certificates of conformity... actually this is an older OIML certificate under the old system; but the schema says scheme (A|B). This cert was issued under the OIML Certificate System (pre-CS), typically scheme... Hmm. The old system corresponds to OIML-CS Scheme A? Actually OIML-CS has Scheme A (certificate of conformity issued by an issuing authority based on test reports from... both involve testing). Scheme A: Issuing Authority issues cert based on test report(s) issued by issuing authorities. Scheme B: Issuing Authority issues cert based on manufacturer's self-declaration... no wait.\n\nOIML-CS: Scheme A — certificates issued on the basis of test reports issued by an issuing authority; Scheme B — certificates issued on the basis of a declaration of conformity from the manufacturer. This certificate is based on tests by NMi, so Scheme A. I'll use \"A\".\n\nAccuracy classes: 1 and 2 (for water meters, accuracy class 1 and 2).\n\nNow the metrological tables. There are multiple tables for different accuracy classes and flow ratio variants:\n\nPage 3 table: Meter size DN65, DN80 (x2 variants), DN100 (x2), DN125 (x2) — Class 1\nPage 5 table: DN150 (x2), DN200, DN250, DN300 — Class 1\nPage 6 table: DN25 (x2), DN40 (x2), DN50 (x2), DN65 (x2) — Class 2, Ratio Q3/Q1 = 400\nPage 7 table: DN80 (x2), DN100 (x2), DN125 (x2), DN150 (x2) — Class 2, Ratio Q3/Q1 = 400\nPage 8 table: DN200, DN250, DN300 — Class 2, Ratio Q3/Q1 = 400\n\nSo the model variants are defined by meter size AND ratio Q3/Q1 (160 or 250) — i.e., each DN size can have different Q3 values leading to different Q1/Q2. Let me parse each variant.\n\nClass 1 (pages 3 and 5):\n\nPage 3 table columns: Meter size: DN65 (1 col), DN80 (2 cols), DN100 (2 cols), DN125 (2 cols). Total 8 columns (label + 7).\n\nRows:\n- Q1: 0.40 | 0.625, 0.64 | 1.0, 1.0 | 1.56, 1.60\n- Q2: 0.64 | 1.00, 1.02 | 1.6, 1.6 | 2.50, 2.56\n- Q3: 100 | 100, 160 | 160, 250 | 250, 400\n- Q4: 125 | 125, 200 | 200, 312.5 | 312.5, 500\n- Ratio Q3/Q1: 250 | 160, 250 | 160, 250 | 160, 250\n- Nominal diameter: 65 | 80 | 100 | 125\n- Accuracy Class: 1 (all)\n- Max admissible pressure: 16 bar (all)\n- Min/max admissible temperature: 0.1/50 °C\n- Indicating range: 99.999.999 m³\n- Verification scale interval: 0.0001 m³ (first 3 columns = DN65, DN80 x2), 0.001 m³ (rest = DN100 x2, DN125 x2)\n- Orientation: All positions\n- Environmental class: C\n- Power supply: Battery 3.6 V\n\nSo class 1 variants:\n1. DN65, Q3=100, Q4=125, Q1=0.40, Q2=0.64, ratio 250, VSI=0.0001\n2. DN80, Q3=100, Q4=125, Q1=0.625, Q2=1.00, ratio 160, VSI=0.0001\n3. DN80, Q3=160, Q4=200, Q1=0.64, Q2=1.02, ratio 250, VSI=0.0001\n4. DN100, Q3=160, Q4=200, Q1=1.0, Q2=1.6, ratio 160, VSI=0.001\n5. DN100, Q3=250, Q4=312.5, Q1=1.0, Q2=1.6, ratio 250, VSI=0.001\n6. DN125, Q3=250, Q4=312.5, Q1=1.56, Q2=2.50, ratio 160, VSI=0.001\n7. DN125, Q3=400, Q4=500, Q1=1.60, Q2=2.56, ratio 250, VSI=0.001\n\nPage 5 table (class 1 continued): DN150 (2 cols), DN200 (1 col), DN250 (1 col), DN300 (1 col). Total 6 columns.\n\nRows:\n- Q1: 2.50, 2.52 | 3.94 | 6.25 | 10\n- Q2: 4.00, 4.03 | 6.30 | 10 | 16\n- Q3: 400 | 630 | 630 | 1000 | 1600\n- Q4: 500 | 787.5 | 787.5 | 1250 | 2000\n- Ratio Q3/Q1: 160 | 250 | 160 | 160 | 160\n- Nominal diameter: 150 | 200 | 250 | 300\n- Accuracy Class: 1\n- Max pressure: 16 (DN150, DN200, DN250 → colspan 3), 10 (DN250? no wait)\n\nHmm, Maximum admissible pressure row: colspan=\"3\" for 16, colspan=\"2\" for 10. Columns: DN150, DN150, DN200 (3 columns → 16 bar); DN250, DN300 (2 columns → 10 bar).\n\n- Temperature: 0.1/50 °C all\n- Indicating range: 99.999.999\n- Verification scale interval: 0.001 (colspan 4 → DN150 x2, DN200, DN250), 0.01 (DN300)\n- Orientation: All positions\n- Environmental class: C\n- Power supply: Battery 3.6 V\n- Software versions: 4.0.4_; 4.0.10_; 4.0.11_; 4.0.12_; 4.2.2_; 4.2.4_\n\nClass 1 continued variants:\n8. DN150, Q3=400, Q4=500, Q1=2.50, Q2=4.00, ratio 160, VSI=0.001, Pmax=16\n9. DN150, Q3=630, Q4=787.5, Q1=2.52, Q2=4.03, ratio 250, VSI=0.001, Pmax=16\n10. DN200, Q3=630, Q4=787.5, Q1=3.94, Q2=6.30, ratio 160, VSI=0.001, Pmax=16\n11. DN250, Q3=1000, Q4=1250, Q1=6.25, Q2=10, ratio 160, VSI=0.001, Pmax=10\n12. DN300, Q3=1600, Q4=2000, Q1=10, Q2=16, ratio 160, VSI=0.01, Pmax=10\n\nClass 2 (pages 6, 7, 8): Ratio Q3/Q1 = 400 for all.\n\nPage 6 table: DN25 (2), DN40 (2), DN50 (2), DN65 (2). 8 data columns.\n- Q1: 0.025, 0.040 | 0.0625, 0.100 | 0.100, 0.1575 | 0.1575, 0.250\n- Q2: 0.040, 0.064 | 0.100, 0.160 | 0.160, 0.252 | 0.250, 0.400\n- Q3: 10, 16 | 25, 40 | 40, 63 | 63, 100\n- Q4: 12.5, 20 | 31.3, 50 | 50, 78.75 | 78.75, 125\n- Ratio: 400 all\n- Nominal diameter: 25 | 40 | 50 | 65\n- Accuracy class: 2\n- Max pressure: 16\n- Temp: 0.1/50\n- Indicating range: 99.999.999\n- VSI: 0.00001 (DN25 x2), 0.0001 (rest)\n- Orientation: All positions\n- Environmental class: C\n- Power supply: Battery 3.6 V\n- Software versions: same list\n\nClass 2 variants:\n13. DN25, Q3=10, Q4=12.5, Q1=0.025, Q2=0.040, VSI=0.00001\n14. DN25, Q3=16, Q4=20, Q1=0.040, Q2=0.064, VSI=0.00001\n15. DN40, Q3=25, Q4=31.3, Q1=0.0625, Q2=0.100, VSI=0.0001\n16. DN40, Q3=40, Q4=50, Q1=0.100, Q2=0.160, VSI=0.0001\n17. DN50, Q3=40, Q4=50, Q1=0.100, Q2=0.160, VSI=0.0001\n18. DN50, Q3=63, Q4=78.75, Q1=0.1575, Q2=0.252, VSI=0.0001\n19. DN65, Q3=63, Q4=78.75, Q1=0.1575, Q2=0.250, VSI=0.0001\n20. DN65, Q3=100, Q4=125, Q1=0.250, Q2=0.400, VSI=0.0001\n\nPage 7 table: DN80 (2), DN100 (2), DN125 (2), DN150 (2).\n- Q1: 0.25, 0.40 | 0.40, 0.625 | 0.625, 1.00 | 1.00, 1.575\n- Q2: 0.40, 0.64 | 0.64, 1.00 | 1.00, 1.60 | 1.60, 2.52\n- Q3: 100, 160 | 160, 250 | 250, 400 | 400, 630\n- Q4: 125, 200 | 200, 312.5 | 312.5, 500 | 500, 787.5\n- Ratio: 400 all\n- Nominal diameter: 80 | 100 | 125 | 150\n- Class 2, Pmax 16, temp 0.1/50\n- Indicating range: 99.999.999\n- VSI: 0.0001 (DN80 x2), 0.001 (rest)\n- Orientation, Env class C, Battery 3.6 V, software versions\n\nClass 2 variants continued:\n21. DN80, Q3=100, Q4=125, Q1=0.25, Q2=0.40, VSI=0.0001\n22. DN80, Q3=160, Q4=200, Q1=0.40, Q2=0.64, VSI=0.0001\n23. DN100, Q3=160, Q4=200, Q1=0.40, Q2=0.64, VSI=0.001\n24. DN100, Q3=250, Q4=312.5, Q1=0.625, Q2=1.00, VSI=0.001\n25. DN125, Q3=250, Q4=312.5, Q1=0.625, Q2=1.00, VSI=0.001\n26. DN125, Q3=400, Q4=500, Q1=1.00, Q2=1.60, VSI=0.001\n27. DN150, Q3=400, Q4=500, Q1=1.00, Q2=1.60, VSI=0.001\n28. DN150, Q3=630, Q4=787.5, Q1=1.575, Q2=2.52, VSI=0.001\n\nPage 8 table: DN200, DN250, DN300 (1 col each).\n- Q1: 1.575 | 2.50 | 4.00\n- Q2: 2.52 | 4.00 | 6.40\n- Q3: 630 | 1000 | 1600\n- Q4: 787.5 | 1250 | 2000\n- Ratio: 400 all\n- Nominal diameter: 200 | 250 | 300\n- Class 2\n- Pmax: 16 (DN200), 10 (DN250, DN300)\n- Temp: 0.1/50\n- Indicating range: 99.999.999\n- VSI: 0.001 (DN200, DN250), 0.01 (DN300)\n- Orientation: All positions\n- Env class C\n- Battery 3.6 V\n- Software versions\n\nClass 2 variants:\n29. DN200, Q3=630, Q4=787.5, Q1=1.575, Q2=2.52, VSI=0.001, Pmax=16\n30. DN250, Q3=1000, Q4=1250, Q1=2.50, Q2=4.00, VSI=0.001, Pmax=10\n31. DN300, Q3=1600, Q4=2000, Q1=4.00, Q2=6.40, VSI=0.01, Pmax=10\n\nTotal: 12 class-1 variants + 19 class-2 variants = 31 variants.\n\nHmm, that's a lot of models. Should I create model entries for each? That's per the schema — model_family with models[] one entry per variant. Let me define model_id as \"DN65-250\" style, maybe \"DN65_Q3/Q1=250\" or \"DN65 (Q3=100 m³/h)\". Let me use something like \"DN65-r250\" — hmm, better to be descriptive: model_id \"DN65 Q3/Q1=250\".\n\nActually the distinguishing axis is: meter size (DN) and Q3 (or ratio Q3/Q1 for class 2... well class 2 has only ratio 400, but two Q3 options per DN). For class 1, each DN has two ratio options (160, 250) with corresponding Q3. For class 2, each DN has two Q3 options (which are the smaller and the next standard Q3) with ratio 400.\n\nSo each variant is uniquely identified by DN + Q3. Let me use model_id like \"DN65 Q3=100\".\n\nFor model attributes: accuracy_class, nominal diameter, q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1, verification_scale_interval, maximum_admissible_pressure.\n\nCommon type-level characteristics:\n- min/max admissible temperature: 0.1/50 °C → {min: 0.1, max: 50}, °C\n- indicating range: 99.999.999 m³ → value 99999999, unit m³\n- orientation: All positions\n- environmental class: C\n- power supply: Battery, 3.6 V → power_supply_type \"Battery\", battery_voltage 3.6 V\n- software versions: 4.0.4_; 4.0.10_; 4.0.11_; 4.0.12_; 4.2.2_; 4.2.4_\n\nNow matrix_tables: I should preserve the tables. I could emit each page's table as a matrix_table, or combine into one. The schema says \"preserve multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere\". Since I'm populating model_family and model_level, maybe I emit matrix_tables for each page table. That could be verbose but faithful. Let me emit 5 matrix tables (one per page) or combine class 1 into one and class 2 into one. I'll emit per-page tables as they appear, with rows per variant column.\n\nActually, I think it's cleaner to emit matrix_tables per source table with rows keyed by variant id. Columns: model, meter_size, q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1, nominal_diameter, accuracy_class, max_admissible_pressure, min_max_admissible_temperature, indicating_range, verification_scale_interval, orientation, environmental_class, power_supply.\n\nHmm, but the schema example shows columns like [\"size\", \"qmax\", ...] and rows as objects with those keys. Let me do that.\n\nAlternatively, since model_level covers the varying attributes, matrix_tables may be redundant. But the rules say detect and extract BOTH matrix_tables AND model_level (per R137 example). I'll include matrix_tables — one combined table per accuracy class might be best, or 5 separate. Let me do 5 separate ones matching the pages, named e.g. \"Metrological characteristics — Class 1, DN65–DN125 (page 3)\".\n\nActually, to keep the JSON manageable, I'll do one matrix table per page (5 tables).\n\nLet me also think about model_id naming. I'll use e.g. \"DN65-Q3=100\" for class 1 and \"DN65-Q3=63\" etc. For class 2, DN50 has Q3=40 and 63; DN65 has 63 and 100.\n\nLet me now define model list:\n\nClass 1 (accuracy_class 1):\n- DN65-Q3=100: q1 0.40, q2 0.64, q3 100, q4 125, ratio 250, dn 65, vsi 0.0001, pmax 16\n- DN80-Q3=100: q1 0.625, q2 1.00, q3 100, q4 125, ratio 160, dn 80, vsi 0.0001, pmax 16\n- DN80-Q3=160: q1 0.64, q2 1.02, q3 160, q4 200, ratio 250, dn 80, vsi 0.0001, pmax 16\n- DN100-Q3=160: q1 1.0, q2 1.6, q3 160, q4 200, ratio 160, dn 100, vsi 0.001, pmax 16\n- DN100-Q3=250: q1 1.0, q2 1.6, q3 250, q4 312.5, ratio 250, dn 100, vsi 0.001, pmax 16\n- DN125-Q3=250: q1 1.56, q2 2.50, q3 250, q4 312.5, ratio 160, dn 125, vsi 0.001, pmax 16\n- DN125-Q3=400: q1 1.60, q2 2.56, q3 400, q4 500, ratio 250, dn 125, vsi 0.001, pmax 16\n- DN150-Q3=400: q1 2.50, q2 4.00, q3 400, q4 500, ratio 160, dn 150, vsi 0.001, pmax 16\n- DN150-Q3=630: q1 2.52, q2 4.03, q3 630, q4 787.5, ratio 250, dn 150, vsi 0.001, pmax 16\n- DN200-Q3=630: q1 3.94, q2 6.30, q3 630, q4 787.5, ratio 160, dn 200, vsi 0.001, pmax 16\n- DN250-Q3=1000: q1 6.25, q2 10, q3 1000, q4 1250, ratio 160, dn 250, vsi 0.001, pmax 10\n- DN300-Q3=1600: q1 10, q2 16, q3 1600, q4 2000, ratio 160, dn 300, vsi 0.01, pmax 10\n\nClass 2 (accuracy_class 2, ratio 400):\n- DN25-Q3=10: q1 0.025, q2 0.040, q3 10, q4 12.5, dn 25, vsi 0.00001, pmax 16\n- DN25-Q3=16: q1 0.040, q2 0.064, q3 16, q4 20, dn 25, vsi 0.00001, pmax 16\n- DN40-Q3=25: q1 0.0625, q2 0.100, q3 25, q4 31.3, dn 40, vsi 0.0001, pmax 16\n- DN40-Q3=40: q1 0.100, q2 0.160, q3 40, q4 50, dn 40, vsi 0.0001, pmax 16\n- DN50-Q3=40: q1 0.100, q2 0.160, q3 40, q4 50, dn 50, vsi 0.0001, pmax 16\n- DN50-Q3=63: q1 0.1575, q2 0.252, q3 63, q4 78.75, dn 50, vsi 0.0001, pmax 16\n- DN65-Q3=63: q1 0.1575, q2 0.250, q3 63, q4 78.75, dn 65, vsi 0.0001, pmax 16\n- DN65-Q3=100: q1 0.250, q2 0.400, q3 100, q4 125, dn 65, vsi 0.0001, pmax 16\n- DN80-Q3=100: q1 0.25, q2 0.40, q3 100, q4 125, dn 80, vsi 0.0001, pmax 16\n- DN80-Q3=160: q1 0.40, q2 0.64, q3 160, q4 200, dn 80, vsi 0.0001, pmax 16\n- DN100-Q3=160: q1 0.40, q2 0.64, q3 160, q4 200, dn 100, vsi 0.001, pmax 16\n- DN100-Q3=250: q1 0.625, q2 1.00, q3 250, q4 312.5, dn 100, vsi 0.001, pmax 16\n- DN125-Q3=250: q1 0.625, q2 1.00, q3 250, q4 312.5, dn 125, vsi 0.001, pmax 16\n- DN125-Q3=400: q1 1.00, q2 1.60, q3 400, q4 500, dn 125, vsi 0.001, pmax 16\n- DN150-Q3=400: q1 1.00, q2 1.60, q3 400, q4 500, dn 150, vsi 0.001, pmax 16\n- DN150-Q3=630: q1 1.575, q2 2.52, q3 630, q4 787.5, dn 150, vsi 0.001, pmax 16\n- DN200-Q3=630: q1 1.575, q2 2.52, q3 630, q4 787.5, dn 200, vsi 0.001, pmax 16\n- DN250-Q3=1000: q1 2.50, q2 4.00, q3 1000, q4 1250, dn 250, vsi 0.001, pmax 10\n- DN300-Q3=1600: q1 4.00, q2 6.40, q3 1600, q4 2000, dn 300, vsi 0.01, pmax 10\n\nNote: class-1 and class-2 have overlapping model IDs (e.g., DN65-Q3=100 exists in both classes with different q1!). Class 1 DN65-Q3=100 has q1=0.40, ratio 250. Class 2 DN65-Q3=100 has q1=0.250, ratio 400. So model_id must include accuracy class! Let me name: \"Class 1 DN65 Q3=100\" and \"Class 2 DN65 Q3=100\". Or include ratio. Better: \"DN65-ratio250\" vs \"DN65-ratio400\" — for class 2, ratio is 400 for all; for class 1, ratio is 160 or 250. Actually including both accuracy class and Q3 is clearest: model_id \"DN65-Q3=100-C1\" hmm ugly.\n\nLet me use: model_id: \"DN65 Q3=100 (class 1)\". Or attributes-based. I'll use model_id like \"Class 1 / DN65 / Q3=100\". Hmm, simpler: \"C1-DN65-Q3=100\". I think readability: \"DN65, class 1, Q3=100\". Let me just go with \"Class1-DN65-Q3=100\".\n\nActually, since accuracy class is itself an attribute in the model entries, I'll add attributes{accuracy_class: 1} to each model. The model_id needs to be unique: I'll use \"DN65-Q3=100-C1\" format. Hmm, let me think of the most natural: The certificate describes variants by \"Meter size\" DN and flow values. I'll use model_id \"DN65-Q3=100\" plus label \"Class 1, DN65, Q3=100 m³/h (Q3/Q1 = 250)\". But duplicate ids across classes would be confusing in model_level values (which reference model by model_id). So make ids unique: append class.\n\nDecision: model_id = e.g. \"C1-DN65-Q3=100\"; label = \"Class 1, DN65, Q3=100 m³/h, Q3/Q1=250\".\n\nHmm, wait — for class 2 models, DN65-Q3=63 class2: q1=0.1575 → ratio 400. And class 1 DN65-Q3=100: ratio 250. So model ids with class prefix work.\n\nModel-level characteristics: I'll emit entries for:\n- nominal_diameter (mm)\n- minimum_flow_rate_q1 (m³/h)\n- transitional_flow_rate_q2 (m³/h)\n- permanent_flow_rate_q3 (m³/h)\n- overload_flow_rate_q4 (m³/h)\n- ratio_q3_q1 (dimensionless)\n- accuracy_class (dimensionless)\n- maximum_admissible_pressure (bar)\n- verification_scale_interval (m³)\n\nEach with 31 values. That's long but correct.\n\nType-level characteristics:\n- minimum_admissible_temperature: 0.1 °C\n- maximum_admissible_temperature: 50 °C\n- Or combined min_max_admissible_temperature: {min: 0.1, max: 50} °C. The table row is \"Min/max admissible temperature (°C) 0,1/50\". I'll emit min_max_admissible_temperature with range value.\n- indicating_range: 99999999 m³ (99.999.999 = dots as thousands separators → 99,999,999). Yes, \"99.999.999\" with dots as thousand separators = 99,999,999. Value: 99999999.\n- orientation: \"All positions\"\n- environmental_class: \"C\"\n- power_supply_type: \"Battery\"\n- battery_voltage: 3.6 V (Ubattery)\n- software_versions: [\"4.0.4\", \"4.0.10\", \"4.0.11\", \"4.0.12\", \"4.2.2\", \"4.2.4\"] — note the trailing underscore in \"4.0.4_\" seems to be part of the version format (placeholder for suffix?). The markdown shows \"4.0.4_; 4.0.10_; ...\". I'll keep as strings \"4.0.4_\" etc.? The underscore likely indicates the software version has a trailing character. I'll record as given: \"4.0.4_\", ... Actually maybe it's OCR of \"4.0.4_x\" where x varies. I'll record the list as-is in a characteristic software_versions with value being array? Value must be number|minmax|string. An array isn't in the shape. Hmm. I could make software_versions a string: \"4.0.4_; 4.0.10_; 4.0.11_; 4.0.12_; 4.2.2_; 4.2.4_\". That keeps shape. Or use components? No. I'll use a single string value.\n\nTest reports:\n- R49-1/2006-NL1-09.01, 41 pages + 14 annexes\n- R49-1/2006-NL1-10.01, 40 pages + 3 annexes\n- R49-1/2006-NL1-11.01, 40 pages + 4 annexes\n- R49-1/2006-NL1-12.01, 40 pages + 3 annexes (note \"R49-1.2006-NL1-12.01\" typo in markdown — normalize to R49-1/2006-NL1-12.01)\n\nPages field: \"41 pages and 14 annexes\" → pages: 41? I could put pages: \"41 pages + 14 annexes\". Schema: id, date, pages, role. No dates given. role: test_report. I'll set pages as string \"41 + 14 annexes\" or number 41. Hmm. I'll use pages: 41 and note annexes... there's no annexes field. Maybe put pages: \"41 pages + 14 annexes\" as string to preserve. The schema says pages — I'll use string to preserve annex info. Actually let me just use the number and mention annexes in role? No. I'll do \"pages\": \"41 (+ 14 annexes)\". Hmm, that might break downstream typing. I'll keep it simple: pages: 41, and add annexes info... The schema is fixed; extra fields maybe okay but risky. I'll go with string preserving both: \"41 pages + 14 annexes\". I think that's acceptable since \"pages\" value type unspecified. Let me choose string.\n\nRevision history: revision \"Initial\", date \"29 November 2012\" → ISO? The schema example: revision (string, e.g. \"0\"), date, changes. Date format — I'll use \"2012-11-29\". Changes: null/omit. The revision is \"Initial\" → I could use \"0\"? Schema says revision string e.g. \"0\". The table says \"Initial\". I'll use \"Initial\" to be faithful. Hmm, \"revision (string, e.g. \\\"0\\\" not 0)\" — just means string type. I'll put \"Initial\".\n\nCertificate number: \"R49-1/2006-NL1-12.03\". Scheme: \"B\"? This is a certificate issued under OIML-CS? The cert is from 2012, before OIML-CS launched (2014?). Under the old \"OIML Certificate System for measuring instruments\" there was one scheme. The schema demands scheme (A|B). Given tests by issuing authority → equivalent to Scheme A. I'll put \"A\".\n\nRecommendation: id \"R49\", edition 2006, amendment null. Note the cert references R49-1/2006, R49-2/2006, R49-3/2006. The certificate is under R49 (Water meters). Parts 1-3. I'll set id \"R49\", edition 2006, and maybe mention parts in scheme? There's a \"scheme\" field in recommendation too? The yaml shows recommendation: id, edition, amendment, scheme, accuracy_classes[]. Hmm, scheme here might mean \"E\" (English)? In many OIML certs the recommendation edition like \"R49-1/2006 (E)\". I'll put scheme: \"E\"? Actually \"scheme\" likely refe